  run_ids: ["70816560-6a44-4102-ab27-53b0c8bd7dfe"] -> ["b6e97d51-e8a5-49f8-9056-2135e8ebf056"]
  explanation: "The unchanged bit_field 0.10.3 single-bit methods are verified for all twelve integer types: in-range indices read or update exactly that bit, returned mutable-reference writes propagate to the original value, and invalid indices fail at the assertion. All twelve primitive BIT_LENGTH values equal their widths. The original generic slice bit_length/get_bit/set_bit bodies are also composition-proved: assuming their declared BitField constant and per-element normal-return contracts, they use element index bit/width and local index bit%width, preserve other slice entries on update, and report zero-width/out-of-bounds errors. bit_length returns length times width or integerOverflow. Rust source and Cargo.toml are archive-identical. Separately, original-Rust Kani checks cover RangeFull specializations of get_bits and set_bits for each of the twelve primitive integer types. For every scalar value, get_bits(..) returns that exact word without changing it. For every receiver and replacement, set_bits(.., replacement) replaces the complete word, returns the mutable reference containing the replacement, and a subsequent arbitrary write through it updates the original word. Signed negative values and sign bits are included. These are twenty-four closed specializations with1284 successful checks, not completion of the generic RangeBounds method families or additional machine mappings." -> "The unchanged bit_field 0.10.3 single-bit methods are verified for all twelve integer types: in-range indices read or update exactly that bit, returned mutable-reference writes propagate to the original value, and invalid indices fail at the assertion. All twelve primitive BIT_LENGTH values equal their widths. The original generic slice bit_length/get_bit/set_bit bodies are also composition-proved: assuming their declared BitField constant and per-element normal-return contracts, they use element index bit/width and local index bit%width, preserve other slice entries on update, and report zero-width/out-of-bounds errors. bit_length returns length times width or integerOverflow. Rust source and Cargo.toml are archive-identical. Separately, original-Rust Kani checks cover RangeFull specializations of get_bits and set_bits for each of the twelve primitive integer types. For every scalar value, get_bits(..) returns that exact word without changing it. For every receiver and replacement, set_bits(.., replacement) replaces the complete word, returns the mutable reference containing the replacement, and a subsequent arbitrary write through it updates the original word. Signed negative values and sign bits are included. These are twenty-four closed specializations with1284 successful checks, not completion of the generic RangeBounds method families or additional machine mappings. Separate kernel-checked supplements cover the original compiler-inlined get_bits/set_bits calls with core::ops::Range<usize> for u8, u16, u32 and u64, for every represented word, replacement and endpoint pair in the checked profile. Valid getters return (word / 2^start) % 2^(end-start); invalid endpoints fail at the assertion. Valid nonempty setters replace exactly the selected bits when the replacement fits and otherwise fail at the assertion. Empty setters preserve the word for replacement zero and fail with integerOverflow for a nonzero replacement; returned-reference writes propagate the supplied writeback value. The usize getter has the same exhaustive result specification under the explicit System.Platform.numBits = 64 premise. Its setter is not verified. These five independent Lean environments supply 145 audited theorem/helper names for nine scoped method specializations, not 145 APIs or new registered claims."
  trusted_assumptions: "The official Rust/Charon/Aeneas translation and integer/slice models, Lean, and Kani/CBMC are trusted. The size_of model uses Lean-only PrimitiveRustLayout evidence with twelve supplied primitive instances, including pointer size8 on x86_64. The original generated Funs is unchanged: each of its twelve ground calls statically elaborates to the expected instance dictionary, checked explicitly. This added implicit evidence is not a Rust trait or an in-Lean proof of std layout. The Lean class remains extensible; coverage is the frozen twelve-call closure, not class sealing. Bool lacks an instance in the audited environment, and there is no supplied fallback for arbitrary types. Kani checks the same twelve actual layouts and all primitive single-bit/returned-reference behavior for arbitrary valid values. Connecting Kani Rust and Lean representations is a reviewed bridge, not an in-Lean std layout proof; Rust nightlies differ. Generic slice theorems assume the complete stated observation/update callback contracts, including the final state after returned-reference writeback. They do not prove arbitrary callback implementations. Mapped audits allow only propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. The RangeFull supplement executes original public methods and actual std size_of under Kani0.68/CBMC6.11/nightly2026-08-21, default debug x86_64 with64-bit usize/isize. Its compiler, standard and validity/panic semantics are trusted; no source adaptation, model, stub or suppressed UB check is introduced. It is independent of the Lean-only layout evidence and supplies no formal cross-tool equivalence." -> "The official Rust/Charon/Aeneas translation and integer/slice models, Lean, and Kani/CBMC are trusted. The size_of model uses Lean-only PrimitiveRustLayout evidence with twelve supplied primitive instances, including pointer size8 on x86_64. The original generated Funs is unchanged: each of its twelve ground calls statically elaborates to the expected instance dictionary, checked explicitly. This added implicit evidence is not a Rust trait or an in-Lean proof of std layout. The Lean class remains extensible; coverage is the frozen twelve-call closure, not class sealing. Bool lacks an instance in the audited environment, and there is no supplied fallback for arbitrary types. Kani checks the same twelve actual layouts and all primitive single-bit/returned-reference behavior for arbitrary valid values. Connecting Kani Rust and Lean representations is a reviewed bridge, not an in-Lean std layout proof; Rust nightlies differ. Generic slice theorems assume the complete stated observation/update callback contracts, including the final state after returned-reference writeback. They do not prove arbitrary callback implementations. Mapped audits allow only propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. The RangeFull supplement executes original public methods and actual std size_of under Kani0.68/CBMC6.11/nightly2026-08-21, default debug x86_64 with64-bit usize/isize. Its compiler, standard and validity/panic semantics are trusted; no source adaptation, model, stub or suppressed UB check is introduced. It is independent of the Lean-only layout evidence and supplies no formal cross-tool equivalence. The standard-range supplements use the same official compiler/Charon/Aeneas toolchain with original bodies inlined into actual UFCS callers. Their original generated and primitive layout provider bytes remain fixed. Each width is elaborated in its own Lean process and module environment, sharing only the pinned official dependency sources/artifacts. Every audit checks theorem declarations and transitive axioms against propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. The usize getter additionally assumes the Lean platform width is64, matching the recorded x86_64 Rust target; this is an explicit configuration premise, not an added axiom or proof about every platform."
  evidence_url: "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/d5937b4cd322bfbb83c581eba1ad17b5fad851fe" -> "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/f3c5ea39b41651fee1dea0e825496a44953cb898"
  limitations: "Only twenty-four primitive single-bit methods, twelve width constants and three callback-conditional slice bodies are established. General primitive and slice get_bits/set_bits range contracts remain unproved; the separate RangeFull-only Kani results described here cover closed primitive specializations. A separately Kani-checked example shows signed range behavior differs from ordinary masked updates: 112_i8.set_bits(0..4,1) yields 1, changing high bits outside the range; the unsigned example yields 113. The verified single-bit methods are unaffected. Generic callback side effects, failure/divergence outside the stated contracts, other targets and release extraction are not established. No generic Rust size_of property, panic text or unwinding-state guarantee is claimed. Signed-range diagnostic evidence: https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/57c4d16e1184f84353db90589bc3cb601f89cb85/batches/2026-11-05-full-public-api/diagnostics/bit-field-signed-range RangeFull denotes the whole primitive word and has no outside bits to preserve; these checks do not imply masked-update behavior for other signed intervals. Arbitrary RangeBounds implementations, bounds callbacks, general intervals, slice BitArray methods, invalid-range panic payload/unwind behavior, other targets or release configuration are not established. The separate signed small-range counterexample remains valid. All27 registered model contracts and their preconditions remain unchanged." -> "The registered primary contracts cover twenty-four primitive single-bit methods, twelve width constants and three callback-conditional slice bodies. General primitive and slice get_bits/set_bits range contracts beyond the scoped specializations described below remain unproved; the separate RangeFull-only Kani results described here cover closed primitive specializations. A separately Kani-checked example shows signed range behavior differs from ordinary masked updates: 112_i8.set_bits(0..4,1) yields 1, changing high bits outside the range; the unsigned example yields 113. The verified single-bit methods are unaffected. Generic callback side effects, failure/divergence outside the stated contracts, other targets and release extraction are not established. No generic Rust size_of property, panic text or unwinding-state guarantee is claimed. Signed-range diagnostic evidence: https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/57c4d16e1184f84353db90589bc3cb601f89cb85/batches/2026-11-05-full-public-api/diagnostics/bit-field-signed-range RangeFull denotes the whole primitive word and has no outside bits to preserve; these checks do not imply masked-update behavior for other signed intervals. Arbitrary RangeBounds implementations, bounds callbacks, other interval specializations, slice BitArray methods, invalid-range panic payload/unwind behavior, other targets or release configuration are not established. The separate signed small-range counterexample remains valid. All27 registered model contracts and their preconditions remain unchanged. The standard-range results concern only these unsigned Range<usize> specializations under the recorded checked/default debug configuration; arbitrary RangeBounds callbacks, signed intervals, other targets and all-input unchecked-release behavior remain outside them. Independent Kani138-check normal-case observations for each of u8 and u16 are distinct from the Lean exhaustive failure proofs; u32/u64/usize add no new Kani run. The usize setter remains blocked by an emitted maximum-usize literal and the official conservative literal bound; no model, literal or IR change is used to bypass it. Global generated Bound-instance collisions prevent a simultaneous import with the primary27 environment, so the five workspaces are checked separately without a nominal-type/model bridge. Result failure and backward-function contracts do not establish Rust panic payloads, unwinding, physical aliasing/heap semantics or arbitrary side effects. The existing signed small-range counterexample and RangeFull24 limits remain in force; generic RangeBounds completion and whole-crate completion are not claimed."
